  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nMid-level ridging is building into the Central and Southern Plains \nthis afternoon ahead of the closed upper low that remains parked off \nthe California coast. The shortwave that brought isolated showers to \nthe area this morning has pushed southeast, leaving dry, sunny, and \nwarm conditions in its wake with temperatures topping out in the \n70s. Light winds and temperatures approaching crossover thresholds \ncould yield patchy fog overnight, although guidance shows some cloud \ncover overhead and modest winds just above the surface which could \npreclude development. Any fog is likely to be confined to low-lying \nareas and would mix out quickly after sunrise. The mid-level ridge \nmoves overhead Thursday into Saturday, keeping dry and unseasonably \nwarm conditions in place with highs around 80 degrees. \n\nA slower ejection of the western trough continues to be the theme \namong guidance; it appears likely that the trough will remain far \nenough west to keep the surface front northwest of the area for \nSunday, creating another dry and warm day. The southern stream \nenergy finally ejects Sunday night into Monday bringing the next \nchance for showers and storms to the area. Guidance varies in speed \nof the wave and precipitation chances linger into Tuesday before the \nwave pushes east and the cold front makes it through the area. Still \nseeing large spreads (15 degrees between NBM 25th-75th percentiles) \nin potential high temperatures Monday into the middle of next week, \nbut temperatures are favored to cool back near climatological norms \nfor late October.",
